        "num_ratings": 11,
        "ratings": [
            {
                "comment": "As every review has said, he's extremely boring. The exams were open note, so an easy grade, but it's really easy to cheat yourself out of learning extremely important EE fundamentals. His lectures are reading out of a textbook for an hour, and then ending class 10 minutes early. Take this with somebody who will make you actually learn the subject.",
                "difficulty_rating": 2,
                "quality_rating": 2
            },
            {
                "comment": "Luke is okay... between him and other 340 profs he might be the least of all evils. His lectures are meh. It's just him pulling out his notes from the first time he taught the class and copying his scratch work up on the board for you to decipher. The exams are just \"find the equation you need, plug-n-chug\" so you might as well just read the book.",
                "difficulty_rating": 2,
                "quality_rating": 2
            },
            {
                "comment": "Not a good teacher. No chance to ask questions.",
                "difficulty_rating": 2,
                "quality_rating": 1
            },
            {
                "comment": "Really boring, hard to stay interested; derivations are a little hard to follow sometimes. That said, he's a nice guy and really not a bad instructor... it's really my fault that I did poorly in his class. If you can do the homework problems, you can do the exams.",
                "difficulty_rating": 3,
                "quality_rating": 4
            },
            {
                "comment": "EXCELLENT",
                "difficulty_rating": 4,
                "quality_rating": 5
            },
            {
                "comment": "Not the most interesting lecturer. If you are able to stay awake, you will find that the concepts are kind of interesting, and Mawst does a good job teaching them.  The tests are very straight forward, and the course is designed in such a way that you can guess pretty easily exactly what will be on the them.",
                "difficulty_rating": 2,
                "quality_rating": 4
            },
            {
                "comment": "Missed many calsses and had TA do the class.  TA would only read from notes (not sure he understood them).  Does not answer questions in class.",
                "difficulty_rating": 2,
                "quality_rating": 1
            },
            {
                "comment": "OK sure, he's not the most dynamic member of the UW faculty.  But this is engineering!  As far as conveying technical information goes, Mawst is great.  He is organized, clearly knows what he is doing and is actually pretty good at answering questions (contrary to other reviews).  I'd definitely recommend him for an advanced course.",
                "difficulty_rating": 3,
                "quality_rating": 4
            },
            {
                "comment": "Speaks in a monotone voice. He is not always clear when defining equations (is this the lattice constant for the substrate, or the crystal&#63;&#63;). \r He does not seem receptive to questions, whether before or during lecture. If you ask a question, he will act as those you're simply being an annoyance. Great course, but take it with a different professor",
                "difficulty_rating": 3,
                "quality_rating": 2
            },
            {
                "comment": "Really boring.  Not particularly hard assignments, but it's extremely difficult to remain interested in the subject when he's teaching.",
                "difficulty_rating": 4,
                "quality_rating": 3
            },
            {
                "comment": "Very boring lectures, and Mawst has pretty much no personality.  However, the subject matter of 335 is pretty dry, so some of this is to be expected.",
                "difficulty_rating": 2,
                "quality_rating": 3
            }
